From 10332b8f891bbeaa21e5aec4e1a01d0f69561ca1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:31:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: himax-hx8279: drop the supplies in the vendor
 order

prepare() brings the rails up one at a time when the panel description
asks for it - I/O rail, iovcc_to_vdd_delay_ms, panel supply - which is
what the vendor power-on list does. power_off() does not mirror that: it
calls regulator_bulk_disable(), and that walks the array backwards, so
the I/O rail goes down first and the panel supply follows with no delay.
The vendor power-off list for gtaxl is the mirror image of its power-on
list: reset low, gpio_buck_en low, 10 ms, then LCD_1P8 off.

Taking the logic supply away while the panel supply is still up leaves
the DriverIC inputs biased through its own I/O, and the part does not
end up in a clean power-on reset. On gtaxlwifi that shows as horizontal
stripes at line pitch on wake-up when the screen has only been off for a
short time: measured over several runs, wake-ups after 1.3 s and 2.3 s
off produce them within a few cycles, while 3.3 s, 10 s and 30 s never
did. A striped wake-up and a good one have byte-identical DSIM and DECON
register dumps and identical logs, and the stripes fade on their own
after some minutes powered down, so what differs is the state the panel
itself came back in.

Take the supplies down in the vendor order, reusing the same delay the
power-on path uses. Panels without a staged power-on keep the bulk call.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-himax-hx8279.c | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-himax-hx8279.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-himax-hx8279.c
index 5f0bba70d2..82df51937f 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-himax-hx8279.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-himax-hx8279.c
@@ -936,7 +936,27 @@ static void hx8279_power_off(struct hx8279 *hx)
 	gpiod_direction_output(hx->reset_gpio, 0);
 	usleep_range(100, 500);
 	gpiod_direction_output(hx->enable_gpio, 0);
-	regulator_bulk_disable(ARRAY_SIZE(hx->vregs), hx->vregs);
+
+	/*
+	 * regulator_bulk_disable() drops the supplies in reverse array
+	 * order, so it takes the I/O rail down first and the panel supply
+	 * immediately after. The vendor power-off list does the opposite
+	 * and waits in between (gpio_buck_en low, 10 ms, LCD_1P8 off),
+	 * which is also the mirror image of the power-on order this
+	 * driver already follows. Removing the logic supply while the
+	 * panel supply is still up leaves the DriverIC inputs biased
+	 * through its own I/O, so a short screen off does not end in a
+	 * clean power-on reset: waking up again within a couple of
+	 * seconds then comes back with horizontal stripes at line pitch,
+	 * while a longer off time is always fine.
+	 */
+	if (hx->desc->iovcc_to_vdd_delay_ms) {
+		regulator_disable(hx->vregs[0].consumer);
+		msleep(hx->desc->iovcc_to_vdd_delay_ms);
+		regulator_disable(hx->vregs[1].consumer);
+	} else {
+		regulator_bulk_disable(ARRAY_SIZE(hx->vregs), hx->vregs);
+	}
 }
 
 static int hx8279_disable(struct drm_panel *panel)
-- 
